(* ============================================================
   Fixed Programming Language — Syntax Grammar
   spec/syntax_grammar.ebnf
   ============================================================

   Status:        DRAFT v0.4.13
   Extracted from: examples/01_basics.fixed .. examples/11_properties.fixed
   Last revised:  2026-11-20

   Refinements in v0.4.13
   ----------------------
   Owner's decisions of 2026-11-20, third round (docs/decisions/P6):
   `UseSel` admits LOWER_IDENT (N7 closed); `SatisfactionRef` may name an
   unnamed or derived satisfaction by its pair; `assume prop` (provisional
   contextual word); `package.fixed` confirmed (N8); naming convention:
   value-level names in camelCase, type-level names in PascalCase
   (spec/stdlib.md §1). The convention is not enforced by the grammar.

   Refinements in v0.4.12
   ----------------------
   Consistency review of 2026-11-20 (amendments in docs/decisions/P6):
   a `using` clause in postfix position (call site, type) names one
   satisfaction or a parenthesised list, so `f(sort(xs) using A, Nil)`
   is not ambiguous; only the top-level `UsingDecl` takes a bare comma
   list. `unreachable` takes an optional string-literal message
   (`AtomExpr`). Interpolation exists only in the message of a
   diagnostic sink (`trace`, `unreachable`), as a name or field path.

   Refinements in v0.4.11
   ----------------------
   Applies decision record P6 (docs/decisions/P6-resolution-round-
   2026-11-20.md, D11–D13).
   - **`pub`** may precede a `fn`, `data`, `cap`, `effect`, type alias or
     `satisfies` declaration at top level (`Visibility`, §2). It is not
     admitted on `use`, on `using` or on a local `fn`. Declarations are
     private by default (spec/type_system.md §8.6).
   - **`using`** is a new reserved word, written in three places. As a
     top-level declaration (`UsingDecl`, §3): `using ByAmount`. As a
     postfix on an application expression (`UsingClause` in `AppExpr`,
     §13): `sort(xs) using ByAmount`. After a type reference (`TypeRef`,
     §10): `Set of Money using ByAmount`. It names satisfactions; see
     spec/type_system.md Rule 8.4.
   - **Named satisfactions**: `Money satisfies Ord named ByAmount:`
     (`SatisfactionName`, §9). `named` is a provisional contextual word,
     recognised only after the capability of a `satisfies` declaration
     or of a `use … satisfies` tail. It is not reserved.
   - **Diagnostics need no production.** `inspect` and `trace` are
     ordinary identifiers that the prelude binds and the compiler
     knows; see the note in §13. Interpolation (`"… {x} …"`) is not
     lexical: see the note at STRING_LITERAL (§1).
   - **Nested `fn` declarations** now have the production the v0.4.8
     entry promised: `BlockStmt` (§13) admits a local `FnDecl` in an
     indented `BlockTail` or `LambdaBody`.
   - Comments that contradicted the productions were corrected (the
     `AppSuffix` note on multi-line lambdas, the `BlockTail` note on
     function bodies). The title no longer carries a second version
     number.
   - `mod` and `in` stay reserved without productions. Packages are
     defined by spec/compilation_model.md §6, not by source syntax.

   Refinements in v0.4.10
   ----------------------
   - **`fip` on capability members.** `FipModifier` may precede a method
     requirement (`InstanceMethodDecl`, `StaticMethodDecl`), where it obliges
     every satisfying type, and a `satisfies` method (`InstanceMethodDef`).
     See spec/memory_management.md Rule MM18.5 and spec/cost.md Rule CT4.3.
   - **`fip(n)`** added: `fip(INT_LITERAL)` allows at most `n` fresh cells
     per call (FP²'s `fip(n)`).
   - **Cost props need no production.** A cost contract is an ordinary
     `PropDecl` whose expression is a comparison with a cost term on the
     left (spec/cost.md Rule CT4.1). `steps`, `constructs`, `releases`,
     `size`, `O`, `log` and `max` are not reserved: they have their cost
     meaning only inside such a prop.
   - No visibility or package syntax yet: `mod` and `pub` stay reserved
     without productions (spec/compilation_model.md OQ-CM1). [`pub`
     received its production in v0.4.11.]

   Refinements in v0.4.9
   ---------------------
   - **`fip` modifier on `fn` declarations** added. `fip fn f(...)` and
     `fip(strict) fn f(...)` declare a checked in-place contract (see
     spec/memory_management.md §18). `fip` is now a reserved word;
     `strict` is contextual and reserved only inside `fip( ... )`.
     The parser accepts the modifier from milestone M10 on
     (docs/plans/phase-3-typer.md §5.7).

   Refinements in v0.4.8
   ---------------------
   - **`.`, `|>`, and `+` clarified as leading-continuation tokens**
     in addition to trailing. The v0.3.1 changelog added `.` to the
     line-continuation set, but only the trailing list was updated;
     `|>` and `+` were always trailing-only. The Leading-continuation
     paragraph now lists all three, matching the idiomatic
     `expr\n   .method()`, `expr\n   |> next`, and `a +\n   b +\n   c`
     styles used throughout the corpus.
   - **`:` joins the dual-mode set** (`=`, `->`, `=>`, `:`). The
     spec already said `cap/data/effect/satisfies/match/handle/do/if/
     else` followed by `:` re-engages the off-side rule inside
     bracket-suppressed regions, but the dual-mode paragraph listed
     only `=`/`->`/`=>`. Without `:`, a `do:` (or `match x:`) that
     opens its body inside parens — common in nested-lambda code —
     would not get an INDENT.
   - **Implicit DEDENT on outer-bracket waiter** (Appendix A.3). When
     a re-engaged off-side body inside a paren-suppressed context
     (lambda body, match block, do block, handler block) is followed
     on the same indent by a token the enclosing bracketed production
     is waiting for (`,` / `)` / `]` / `}`), the body terminates at
     that token; the DEDENT the scanner emits at the next less-
     indented line is absorbed by the enclosing production. Without
     this, code like
       `f(\n    (x) ->\n        body,\n    otherArg)`
     would over-extend the lambda body block past the comma. (Closely
     analogous to Python's behaviour inside parentheses.)
   - **Nested `fn` declarations** are admissible as block statements.
     A block element is either an `Expr` or a local `fn name(...) =
     body` declaration; the latter introduces a name visible in the
     remainder of the block.

   Refinements in v0.4.7
   ---------------------
   - **Implementation appendix added** (§A) defining error-recovery
     anchors and the trivia retention contract. The grammar productions
     are unchanged; the appendix exists so that two conformant Fixed
     parsers cannot disagree on what an erroneous file means or whether
     comments are observable. See `docs/plans/phase-2.1-incremental-
     parser.md` for the bootstrap implementation.

   Refinements in v0.4.6
   ---------------------
   - **Prefix unary operators** added: `UnaryExpr = ("-" | "!")? AppExpr`,
     positioned in the precedence chain between `MulExpr` and `AppExpr`
     (tighter than `*`/`/`/`%`, looser than method/application).
     Single-arity: `--x` and `!!x` not admitted. Both desugar via cap
     methods — `-x ≡ x.negate` (`cap Numeric`), `!x ≡ x.not`
     (`cap Boolean`). See `spec/stdlib.md` §4.9 / §4.5 for the method
     declarations and operator desugaring tables.

   Refinements in v0.4.5
   ---------------------
   - **`CapType` admitted as a `TypeAtom`** — `cap of T` (and
     `cap extends C`) may now appear in any TypeExpr position, not just
     as the leading branch of `FnReturnType`. This is what enables
     higher-order parameter types like `(N, N) -> cap of N`. See
     spec/type_system.md §6.3 for the use-site/declaration-site
     equivalence. The `FnReturnType` production is retained as
     legacy/preferred form for direct return-type writing; both shapes
     parse and mean the same thing.
   - **`ParenTypeApp` added to `TypeAtom`** — `( TypeExpr ) ( "of" OfArg )?`
     allows the explicit-binding HKT-application form
     `(M is Monad) of (is List of B)`. Without parens,
     `M is Monad of B` parses as `M is (Monad of B)` (the existing
     greedy CapRef rule); the parens force the outer `of` to apply to
     the named alias as a constructor.

   Refinements in v0.4.4
   ---------------------
   - **`suchThat` clause** in `forall` quantifiers — a generator-side
     filter for property-based testing: `forall (s: Self) suchThat
     s.size > 0 -> ...`. See spec/properties.md §5.4. `suchThat` is a
     reserved word.

   Refinements in v0.4.3
   ---------------------
   - **Or-patterns** in match cases: `Pat1 | Pat2 => body`. Alternatives
     must bind identical sets of names at identical types. (See
     spec/pattern_matching.md §5.5.)
   - **Guards** in match cases: `Pat if cond => body`. The guard is a
     `bool` expression evaluated after the pattern matches; if false,
     control falls through to the next case. (See §5.6.)
   - **`..` field elision** in struct-destructure patterns:
     `let Pair { first: x, .. } = p` ignores unmentioned fields.
     (See §3.6.)

   Refinements in v0.4.2
   ---------------------
   - **`linear` modifier on `effect` declarations** added. `linear effect E:`
     declares a linear effect whose ops are used at most once per code path
     (see spec/effects.md §3.5). `linear` is now a reserved word.

   Refinements in v0.4.1
   ---------------------
   - **`phantom` keyword removed.** A type parameter is *phantom* iff
     it does not appear in any constructor field, method signature,
     inherited member, or prop expression — this property is inferred
     by the typer rather than declared at the source level. See
     spec/type_system.md §5.9 for the inference rule. The grammar
     `OfArg` no longer has a `"phantom" UPPER_IDENT` alternative;
     `phantom` is no longer a reserved word.

   Major changes in v0.4 — fn/method/lambda consistency
   ----------------------------------------------------
   Goal: consistent shape for every "function-like" declaration. Two
   markers do separate jobs without overlap inside a single context:

     `=`  — *definitions*: introduces the right-hand side of a fn/method
            body (`fn f(x) -> T = body`), a let binding, a type alias,
            a parameter default. Definitions: this thing IS that.
     `:`  — *blocks of declarations or branches*: cap/data/effect/
            satisfies bodies, match/handle/do/if/else branches.

   Concrete changes:
   - **fn / method body introducer changed from `:` to `=`**
     (`fn name(...) -> T = body` instead of `... -> T: body`).
   - **Cap instance and static methods may carry optional bodies**.
     A method declared with a body acts as a *default implementation* /
     *extension method* on every type satisfying the cap; the satisfying
     type can override it via an explicit `satisfies`-block method.
   - **EffectMember gains `WithClause?` and `TypeParamsHint?`**, matching
     `InstanceMethodDecl` exactly.
   - **LambdaParam gains optional type annotation and default value**:
     `(x: i64 = 0) -> body`. The lambda body is still introduced by `->`.
   - **`InstanceMethodDef`** (in satisfies blocks) gains
     `TypeParamsHint?` and `WithClause?`, and accepts a multi-line
     `BlockTail` after `=` (was a single `Expr`).
   - **TupleExpr / TuplePattern** simplified to one alternative each
     using the same shape as `ListLiteralExpr`.

   The continuation-token list now treats `=` as a dual-mode token
   (trailing continuation OR block introducer for indented multi-line
   fn bodies) symmetric to `->` and `=>`.

   Refinements in v0.3.1
   ---------------------
   - `.` added to line-continuation operators: chained method calls
     (`.concat(…).concat(…)` over multiple physical lines) parse as a
     single logical line.
   - Multi-line lambda bodies use `->` as a block introducer. The
     canonical multi-line form is `(x) ->` followed by an indented
     body, replacing `(x) -> do: …`.
   - Multi-line case and clause bodies use `=>` as a block introducer (match cases
     and handler clauses). Replaces the previous brace-block form.
   - Bracket-suppression rule clarified: inside `(…)`/`[…]`/`{…}` the
     off-side rule is suppressed by default, but is re-enabled within
     a lambda body (after `->`), a case or clause body (after `=>`), or a
     declaration/control-flow body (after `:`).

   Major change in v0.3
   --------------------
   **Meaningful whitespace.** Blocks are introduced by `:` and delimited
   by INDENT / DEDENT tokens emitted by the lexer (Python-style off-side
   rule). All `{ … }` block forms are removed. Braces are now reserved
   exclusively for **struct literals** (`Pair { first: x, second: y }`)
   and **use-group imports** (`use a.b.{X, Y}`).

   Block lambdas (`{ x -> body }`) are removed. Use arrow lambdas
   `(x) -> expr` (single-line) or `(x) ->` followed by an indented
   block (multi-line).

   Handle subjects are single expressions. For multi-line subjects, use
   a parenthesised do-block: `handle (do: …): …`.

   Empty cap / data / satisfies bodies omit the `:` entirely:
       cap Locked
       cap Open

   Changes from v0.2.4 (history retained for context)
   --------------------------------------------------
     v0.2.4: UseSel tightened to UPPER_IDENT (no LOWER_IDENT).
     v0.2.3: Documentation-only resolutions for Q8/Q10/Q11/Q16/N1(a)/N1(b).
     v0.2.2: N1 resolved as interpretation B (of value-params are
             type-level indices); OfArg accepts LiteralExpr.
     v0.2.1: N2 resolved (`forall` to PropExpr); N3 resolved (`Self` is
             a keyword).
     v0.2.0: Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4/Q5/Q7/Q9/Q12/Q13/Q14/Q15/Q16 resolved.

   Scope
   -----
   Defines the *syntactic* surface of Fixed. Semantic well-formedness
   lives in sibling spec docs (type_system.md, quantities.md, …).

   Conventions
   -----------
     =       definition
     |       alternation
     ?       optional (zero or one)
     *       zero or more
     +       one or more
     ( )     grouping
     " "     literal terminal
     UPPER   token class (produced by the scanner / indent-tracker)
     Pascal  parser-level nonterminal
     (* ... *)   comment

   Indentation, NEWLINE, INDENT, DEDENT
   ------------------------------------
   The scanner tracks an indent stack that begins with [0]. At the start
   of each logical line, the leading whitespace is measured (in spaces;
   tabs are an error in v0.3).

     - leading whitespace == stack top  → emit NEWLINE
     - leading whitespace >  stack top  → push, emit INDENT
     - leading whitespace <  stack top  → pop until match, emit one
                                          DEDENT per pop

   Inside parentheses `(...)`, brackets `[...]`, or struct-literal
   braces `{...}`, indentation is **suppressed by default** — the
   scanner emits no NEWLINE / INDENT / DEDENT until the bracketing
   closes. This permits wrapped argument lists, list literals, and
   struct literals.

   **Re-enabling the off-side rule inside brackets.** The off-side rule
   is re-enabled within the body of:
     - a fn/method definition following `=` (fn body);
     - a lambda following `->` (lambda body);
     - a match case or handler clause following `=>` (case or clause body); or
     - a cap/data/effect/satisfies/match/handle/do/if/else following `:`
       (block body).
   After the matching DEDENT closes the body, the surrounding bracket
   suppression resumes.

   A logical line may also continue across a physical newline via one
   of two rules:

   **Trailing continuation.** If the line ends with one of the following
   tokens, the next physical line continues the same logical line:
     `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `&&`, `||`, `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`,
     `>=`, `|>`, `->`, `=>`, `=`, `<-`, `.`, `is`, `extends`, `implies`.

   The dual-mode tokens `=`, `->`, `=>`, `:` are block-introducers
   that re-engage the off-side rule even inside parens. After one of
   them, the scanner peeks at the next physical line: if it is
   indented more than the line containing the dual-mode token, INDENT
   is emitted and the off-side rule applies to the body. Otherwise
   the line continues normally. (`=`, `->`, `=>` are also trailing-
   continuation tokens; `:` is solely a block-introducer.)

   **Leading continuation.** If the next physical line begins with one
   of the following tokens (at any indent), it continues the previous
   logical line:
     `with`, `extends`, `->`, `.`, `|>`, `+`.

   `.`, `|>`, and `+` are continuation tokens in *both* leading and
   trailing position: chained method calls, pipelines, and arithmetic
   sums may be split with the operator at either end-of-line or
   start-of-line. Both shapes parse as a single logical line. (Cap
   composition `+` only appears in `is`-bound and `extends` positions,
   so leading `+` in expression context is unambiguous.)

   The continuation rules apply when the next physical line is *not*
   indented more than the current line — that case engages the off-side
   rule and emits INDENT instead. So `(x) ->` followed by a more-
   indented next line opens a multi-line lambda body, while `a +` or
   `e.foo` followed by an equally- or less-indented next line continues
   the same logical line. Multi-line function signatures are written:

       fn participant(name: String) -> ()
           with Console + Channel of (is Message) + Async:
           ...body...

   Comments
   --------
   `//` to end-of-line. Comments are discarded by the scanner before
   indent measurement.
   ============================================================ *)


(* ============================================================
   1. Lexical (token) classes
   ============================================================ *)

LOWER_IDENT     = (* [a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]* *) ;
UPPER_IDENT     = (* [A-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*   *) ;

INT_LITERAL     = (* digits, optional `_` as visual separator *) ;
FLOAT_LITERAL   = (* digits "." digits ( ("e"|"E") ("+"|"-")? digits )? *) ;
STRING_LITERAL  = (* '"' { strchar | escape } '"' *) ;
(* Interpolation is not lexical. `{` and `}` are ordinary characters in
   every string literal (examples/04_json.fixed writes "{}"). When a
   string literal is the message of a diagnostic sink — the argument of
   `trace` or the message of `unreachable` (spec/diagnostics.md Rule
   DG3.2) — the compiler reads
   `{ LOWER_IDENT ( "." LOWER_IDENT )* }` inside it as an interpolated
   name or field path, and `{{` and `}}` as literal braces. Only a path
   is admitted, not a general Expr, so that a string literal never
   needs nested lexing. There is no other string interpolation: in any
   other string literal `{x}` is three characters. *)
CHAR_LITERAL    = (* "'" ( char | escape ) "'"  *) ;
BOOL_LITERAL    = "true" | "false" ;
UNIT_LITERAL    = "(" ")" ;

NEWLINE         = (* end of a logical line; see indentation rules above *) ;
INDENT          = (* synthetic token: opening of a more-indented block *) ;
DEDENT          = (* synthetic token: closing of an indented block *) ;

(* KEYWORDS:
     as      cap         data        do          effect      else
     extends false       fip         fn          forall      handle
     if      implies     impossible  in          is          let
     linear  match       mod         of          prop        pub
     resume  return      Self        satisfies   suchThat    true
     type    unreachable use         using       with                      *)
(* `phantom` removed in v0.4.1 — phantom-ness is now inferred from usage.
   `linear` added in v0.4.2 — modifier on `effect` declarations.
   `suchThat` added in v0.4.4 — filter clause in `forall` quantifiers.
   `fip` added in v0.4.9 — modifier on `fn` declarations.
   `using` added in v0.4.11 — satisfaction choice (spec/type_system.md
   Rule 8.4). It is a separate token from `use`: the scanner takes the
   longest identifier, so `using` never lexes as `use` followed by `ing`.
   `mod` and `in` are reserved without productions.
   Contextual words, not reserved: `strict` (inside `fip( … )`), `named`
   (provisional, after the capability of a `satisfies`). `inspect` and
   `trace` are ordinary identifiers. *)


(* ============================================================
   2. Compilation unit
   ============================================================ *)

CompilationUnit
    = ( NEWLINE | TopItem )*
    ;

TopItem
    = UseDecl
    | UsingDecl
    | Visibility? ( CapDecl
                  | EffectDecl
                  | DataDecl
                  | TypeAlias
                  | FnDecl
                  | SatisfiesDecl )
    ;

(* v0.4.11. Declarations are private to their package by default;
   `pub` makes one public (spec/type_system.md §8.6). `pub` comes before
   any other modifier: `pub fip fn`, `pub linear effect`. *)
Visibility
    = "pub"
    ;


(* ============================================================
   3. Use declarations (imports)
   ============================================================ *)

UseDecl
    = "use" UsePath UseTail?
    ;

UsePath
    = LOWER_IDENT ( "." LOWER_IDENT )* ( "." UPPER_IDENT )?
    ;

UseTail
    = "." "{" UseSel ( "," UseSel )* ","? "}"      (* use-group: not a block, brace-grouped *)
    | "satisfies" CapRef SatisfactionName?          (* use a.b.T satisfies C [named N] *)
    ;

(* A named satisfaction is imported like a type: `use shop.money.ByAmount`
   (a UsePath ending in UPPER_IDENT). *)
(* v0.4.13: a selector may be a lower-case name, so that a `pub fn` of
   another package can be imported by name: `use shop.money.{Money, parseAmount}`.
   A UsePath whose last segment is a LOWER_IDENT names a package or a
   function; the namer decides which. *)
UseSel  = UPPER_IDENT | LOWER_IDENT ;

(* v0.4.11. A top-level `using` names satisfactions that decide, for this
   file, which satisfaction of a pair a site uses (spec/type_system.md
   Rule 8.4, level 3; in the application root, level 5). Each name must
   be a named satisfaction in scope. `use` imports a name; `using`
   chooses a satisfaction. The two never start the same production. *)
UsingDecl
    = "using" SatisfactionRef ( "," SatisfactionRef )*
    ;

(* A named satisfaction by its name; an unnamed or derived one by its
   pair (`using Money satisfies Ord`), v0.4.13. *)
SatisfactionRef
    = UPPER_IDENT ( "satisfies" CapRef )?
    ;


(* ============================================================
   4. Capability declarations
   ============================================================ *)

CapDecl
    = "cap" UPPER_IDENT CapValueParams? CapOfParams? CapExtends? CapBody?
    ;

CapValueParams
    = "(" ValueParam ( "," ValueParam )* ","? ")"
    ;

CapOfParams
    = "of" OfArgList
    ;

CapExtends
    = "extends" CapBound ( "+" CapBound )*
    ;

(* `cap Foo:` introduces an indented body of members. Empty caps omit
   the `:` entirely (`cap Locked`, `cap Meters`). *)
CapBody
    = ":" CapMemberList
    ;

CapMemberList
    = CapMember                                              (* inline single member *)
    | NEWLINE INDENT CapMember ( NEWLINE CapMember )* NEWLINE? DEDENT
    ;

CapMember
    = InstanceMethodDecl
    | StaticMethodDecl
    | PropDecl
    ;

(* InstanceMethodDecl and StaticMethodDecl may carry an optional body
   (v0.4). When present, the body acts as a *default implementation* /
   *extension method* on every type satisfying the cap; satisfying
   types may override it via an explicit `satisfies`-block method. *)
InstanceMethodDecl
    = FipModifier? "fn" LOWER_IDENT TypeParamsHint? FnParamList? "->" TypeExpr WithClause? ( "=" BlockTail )?
    ;

StaticMethodDecl
    = FipModifier? "Self" "." "fn" LOWER_IDENT TypeParamsHint? FnParamList? "->" TypeExpr WithClause? ( "=" BlockTail )?
    ;

TypeParamsHint
    = "<" UPPER_IDENT ( "," UPPER_IDENT )* ","? ">"
    ;


(* ============================================================
   5. Effect declarations
   ============================================================ *)

(* Effects may carry an optional `linear` modifier (v0.4.2). A linear
   effect's ops contribute quantity 1 to the row; total usage across
   all reachable paths must be ≤ 1. See spec/effects.md §3.5. *)
EffectDecl
    = "linear"? "effect" UPPER_IDENT CapOfParams? EffectBody?
    ;

EffectBody
    = ":" EffectMemberList
    ;

EffectMemberList
    = EffectMember
    | NEWLINE INDENT EffectMember ( NEWLINE EffectMember )* NEWLINE? DEDENT
    ;

EffectMember
    = "fn" LOWER_IDENT TypeParamsHint? FnParamList? "->" TypeExpr WithClause?
    ;     (* return type may be `!` *)


(* ============================================================
   6. Data declarations
   ============================================================ *)

DataDecl
    = "data" UPPER_IDENT DataParams? DataBody?
    ;

DataParams
    = "of" OfArgList
    ;

(* DataBody has two forms:
     - colon-introduced: `data Color:` followed by an indented block of
       variants and prop decls.
     - paren-form (single-ctor sugar): `data Config(host: String, ...)`
*)
DataBody
    = ":" DataBodyItemList
    | "(" FieldDecl ( "," FieldDecl )* ","? ")"
    ;

DataBodyItemList
    = DataBodyItem
    | NEWLINE INDENT DataBodyItem ( NEWLINE DataBodyItem )* NEWLINE? DEDENT
    ;

DataBodyItem
    = Variant
    | PropDecl
    ;

Variant
    = UPPER_IDENT ( "(" FieldDecl ( "," FieldDecl )* ","? ")" )?
    ;

FieldDecl
    = LOWER_IDENT ":" TypeExpr ( "=" Expr )?
    ;


(* ============================================================
   7. Type aliases
   ============================================================ *)

TypeAlias
    = "type" UPPER_IDENT TypeAliasParams? "=" TypeAliasRhs
    ;

TypeAliasParams
    = "(" ValueParam ( "," ValueParam )* ","? ")"
    ;

TypeAliasRhs
    = CapBound ( "+" CapBound )*
    ;


(* ============================================================
   8. Function declarations
   ============================================================ *)

FnDecl
    = FipModifier? "fn" LOWER_IDENT TypeParamsHint? FnParamList? "->" FnReturnType WithClause? FnBody
    ;

(* Checked in-place contract (v0.4.9); see spec/memory_management.md §18.
   `strict` is a contextual word, recognised only here. `fip(n)` (v0.4.10)
   allows at most `n` fresh cells per call. The modifier may also precede a
   capability method requirement and a `satisfies` method (v0.4.10). *)
FipModifier
    = "fip" ( "(" ( "strict" | INT_LITERAL ) ")" )?
    ;

FnParamList
    = "(" ( FnParam ( "," FnParam )* ","? )? ")"
    ;

FnParam
    = LOWER_IDENT ":" TypeExpr ( "=" Expr )?
    ;

FnReturnType
    = "cap" CapReturnSpec?
    | TypeExpr
    ;

CapReturnSpec
    = "of" OfArg
    | "extends" CapBound ( "+" CapBound )*
    ;

WithClause
    = "with" EffectRow
    ;

EffectRow
    = EffectBound ( "+" EffectBound )*
    ;

EffectBound
    = UPPER_IDENT ( "of" OfArg )?
    ;

(* Function body is `=`-introduced (v0.4). *)
FnBody
    = "=" BlockTail
    ;


(* ============================================================
   9. Satisfies declarations
   ============================================================ *)

SatisfiesDecl
    = SatisfiesSubject "satisfies" CapRef SatisfactionName? SatisfiesBody?
    ;

(* v0.4.11. `named` is a provisional contextual word (P6 D12; open as
   spec/compilation_model.md OQ-CM14). It is recognised only here and in
   UseTail, right after the CapRef. `CapRef`'s optional `of OfArg` cannot
   absorb it: an OfArg that is a TypeExpr ends at a LOWER_IDENT that is
   not followed by `(`. *)
SatisfactionName
    = "named" UPPER_IDENT
    ;

SatisfiesSubject
    = UPPER_IDENT ( "of" "Self" )?
    | LOWER_IDENT                     (* primitives: u64, f64, ... *)
    ;

SatisfiesBody
    = ":" SatisfiesItemList
    ;

SatisfiesItemList
    = SatisfiesItem
    | NEWLINE INDENT SatisfiesItem ( NEWLINE SatisfiesItem )* NEWLINE? DEDENT
    ;

SatisfiesItem
    = SatisfiesMap
    | InstanceMethodDef
    ;

SatisfiesMap
    = SatisfiesLhs "as" LOWER_IDENT
    ;

SatisfiesLhs
    = UPPER_IDENT
    | "Self"
    | "impossible"
    ;

(* Satisfies-block method definition. Same shape as FnDecl modulo the
   `fn` keyword position; return type is optional (inferred from the
   cap method declaration when omitted). *)
InstanceMethodDef
    = FipModifier? "fn" LOWER_IDENT TypeParamsHint? FnParamList? ( "->" TypeExpr )? WithClause? "=" BlockTail
    ;


(* ============================================================
   10. Type expressions
   ============================================================ *)

TypeExpr
    = ArrowType
    ;

ArrowType
    = ArrowLhs ( "->" ArrowType WithClause? )?
    ;

ArrowLhs
    = "(" ArrowLhsList? ")"
    | TypeAtom
    ;

ArrowLhsList
    = TypeExpr ( "," TypeExpr )* ","?
    ;

TypeAtom
    = "!"
    | "Self" SelfOf?
    | IsBound
    | NamedAlias
    | TypeRef
    | CapType
    | ParenTypeApp
    ;

SelfOf
    = "of" OfArg
    ;

NamedAlias
    = UPPER_IDENT "is" CapBoundChain
    ;

(* `cap of T` and `cap extends C` admitted as TypeAtoms — needed for
   higher-order positions where a function returns a cap, e.g.
   `(N, N) -> cap of N` as a parameter type. The same form is reachable
   via FnReturnType when used directly as a function's return type. *)
CapType
    = "cap" CapReturnSpec?
    ;

(* Explicit-binding HKT application: a parenthesized NamedAlias (or any
   TypeExpr) followed by `of` to apply it as a type constructor. Use:
       fn mapM(...) -> (M is Monad) of (is List of B) = ...
   Without the parens, `M is Monad of B` greedily binds the `of` inside
   the cap-bound (CapRef), giving the different meaning "M satisfies
   `Monad of B`". The parens force outer application. *)
ParenTypeApp
    = "(" TypeExpr ")" "of" OfArg
    ;

IsBound
    = "is" CapBoundChain
    ;

CapBoundChain
    = CapBound ( "+" CapBound )*
    ;

CapBound
    = CapRef
    | RefinementCall
    | PrimitiveType
    ;

CapRef
    = UPPER_IDENT ( "of" OfArg )?
    ;

RefinementCall
    = LOWER_IDENT "(" ArgList? ")"
    ;

PrimitiveType
    = LOWER_IDENT
    | UNIT_LITERAL
    ;

(* v0.4.11. A `using` clause after a type reference names the
   satisfactions that serve the bounds of that type expression:
   `Set of Money using ByAmount`. Like `of`, it binds to the innermost
   TypeRef, so the line above parses as `Set of (Money using ByAmount)`.
   The position does not change the meaning: each name determines its
   (type, capability) pair, and the clause covers the whole type
   expression it is written in (spec/type_system.md Rule 8.4). Since
   v0.4.12 a list of names here needs parentheses, so that
   `Tuple of (Money using A, Money)` names one satisfaction and has two
   arguments. *)
TypeRef
    = UPPER_IDENT ( "of" OfArg )? UsingClause?
    | PrimitiveType
    ;

OfArgList
    = "(" OfArg ( "," OfArg )* ","? ")"
    | OfArg
    ;

(* OfArg admits, at the same syntactic position:
     - value-param declarations with optional type and default;
     - literal value expressions at application sites
       (`Bounded of (i64, 5, 10)`);
     - bare type expressions (declaration or application).
   The parser disambiguates on the next token after the leading IDENT:
     - `IDENT : ...`        → OfValueParam with type
     - `IDENT = ...`        → OfValueParam with default but no type
     - LITERAL              → LiteralExpr
     - otherwise            → TypeExpr.
   Phantom-ness of type parameters is inferred from usage (see
   spec/type_system.md §5.9), not declared via syntax. *)
OfArg
    = OfValueParam
    | LiteralExpr
    | TypeExpr
    ;

OfValueParam
    = ( LOWER_IDENT | UPPER_IDENT ) ":" TypeExpr ( "=" Expr )?
    | ( LOWER_IDENT | UPPER_IDENT ) "=" Expr
    ;

ValueParam
    = ( LOWER_IDENT | UPPER_IDENT ) ( ":" TypeExpr )? ( "=" Expr )?
    ;


(* ============================================================
   11. Property declarations
   ============================================================ *)

(* v0.4.13. `assume` is a provisional contextual word, recognised only
   directly before `prop`. It gives the prop the status `assumed`
   (spec/properties.md Rule P7.7): the programmer vouches for it. *)
PropDecl
    = "assume"? "prop" LOWER_IDENT ":" PropExpr
    ;

PropExpr
    = ImpliesExpr
    | ForallExpr
    | Expr
    ;

ImpliesExpr
    = OrExpr "implies" PropExpr
    ;


(* ============================================================
   12. Patterns
   ============================================================ *)

Pattern
    = "_"
    | LOWER_IDENT
    | LiteralPattern
    | DataVariantPattern
    | TuplePattern
    | StructDestructurePattern
    ;

LiteralPattern
    = INT_LITERAL | FLOAT_LITERAL | STRING_LITERAL | CHAR_LITERAL | BOOL_LITERAL | UNIT_LITERAL
    ;

DataVariantPattern
    = ( UPPER_IDENT | "Self" ) "." UPPER_IDENT ( "(" PatternList? ")" )?
    | UPPER_IDENT ( "(" PatternList? ")" )?
    ;

(* TuplePattern (v0.4) — single production matching TupleExpr's shape.
   Mandatory first comma disambiguates `(p,)` from a parenthesised
   pattern. *)
TuplePattern
    = "(" Pattern "," ( Pattern ( "," Pattern )* ","? )? ")"
    ;

(* StructDestructurePattern (v0.4.3): may end with `..` to ignore
   unmentioned fields. The `..` may appear alone (`Foo { .. }`) or
   after named field patterns (`Foo { x, y, .. }`). *)
StructDestructurePattern
    = UPPER_IDENT "{" StructFieldList? "}"
    ;

StructFieldList
    = ".."
    | FieldPattern ( "," FieldPattern )* ( "," ".." )? ","?
    ;

FieldPattern
    = LOWER_IDENT ( ":" Pattern )?
    ;

PatternList
    = Pattern ( "," Pattern )* ","?
    ;


(* ============================================================
   13. Expressions
   ============================================================
   Operator precedence (low → high):
     pipe          |>
     logical or    ||
     logical and   &&
     comparison    == != < > <= >=
     additive      + -                     (binary)
     multiplicative * / %
     unary         -  !                     (prefix; single-arity, v0.4.5)
     application/method  f(args)  e.m(args)  e.field
     atom

   Unary `-` and `!` are tighter than `*`/`/`/`%` so that `-a * b`
   parses as `(-a) * b`, matching the convention in C/Rust/Scala.
   Per UnaryExpr's definition, only one prefix operator may apply per
   atom; iterate via parens when needed.

   `if`, `match`, `handle`, `do`, lambdas, struct literals, list
   literals, tuples, and `unreachable` all act as atoms.

   `forall` and `implies` are reachable only via PropExpr (Q5 / N2).
   ============================================================ *)

Expr
    = LetExpr
    | PropDecl
    | PipeExpr
    ;

LetExpr                                (* returns () *)
    = "let" Pattern "=" PipeExpr
    ;

(* ForallExpr is reachable only inside PropExpr (Q5/N2). The optional
   `suchThat Expr` clause (v0.4.4) filters generated values in PBT —
   only values satisfying the clause count toward the iteration budget.
   In static proof, `forall (xs) suchThat C -> body` is logically
   equivalent to `forall (xs) -> C implies body`. See
   spec/properties.md §5.4. *)
ForallExpr
    = "forall" "(" Binder ( "," Binder )* ","? ")" SuchThatClause? "->" PropExpr
    ;

SuchThatClause
    = "suchThat" Expr
    ;

Binder
    = LOWER_IDENT ":" TypeExpr
    ;

PipeExpr
    = OrExpr ( "|>" OrExpr )*
    ;

OrExpr
    = AndExpr ( "||" AndExpr )*
    ;

AndExpr
    = CmpExpr ( "&&" CmpExpr )*
    ;

CmpExpr
    = AddExpr ( CmpOp AddExpr )?
    ;

CmpOp
    = "==" | "!=" | "<" | ">" | "<=" | ">="
    ;

AddExpr
    = MulExpr ( ( "+" | "-" ) MulExpr )*
    ;

MulExpr
    = UnaryExpr ( ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) UnaryExpr )*
    ;

(* Prefix unary operators (v0.4.5): single-arity prefix `-` (numeric
   negation) and `!` (logical not). Both desugar via cap methods —
   `-x ≡ x.negate` (cap Numeric), `!x ≡ x.not` (cap Boolean). The unary
   form is single-arity: `--x` and `!!x` are not admitted; programs
   needing iterated negation use parens (`-(-x)`) or call the underlying
   methods directly. *)
UnaryExpr
    = ( "-" | "!" )? AppExpr
    ;

(* v0.4.11. A trailing `using` clause names the satisfactions used by
   the resolutions inside this application expression, its arguments
   included (spec/type_system.md Rule 8.4, level 1). It binds tighter
   than every binary operator: `sort(xs) using ByAmount |> f` applies
   `using` to `sort(xs)` only. To give an operator site a satisfaction,
   write the method call (`a.compare(b) using ByAmount`). `using` cannot
   start an expression, so the clause is not ambiguous with a following
   argument or statement.

   A list of satisfactions in this position needs parentheses
   (`sort(xs) using (ByAmount, ByLen)`): a bare comma would be read as
   the next argument or tuple element (`f(sort(xs) using A, Nil)`).

   `inspect(x)` and `trace("…")` need no production. `inspect` and
   `trace` are LOWER_IDENT atoms applied by AppSuffix; the prelude
   declares them (spec/stdlib.md), the compiler knows those two
   declarations, and a user declaration of the same name shadows them
   and is an ordinary function (examples/05_phantom_types.fixed
   declares its own `fn inspect`). `unreachable` has its own production
   in AtomExpr, with an optional string-literal message. Their meaning
   is given by spec/diagnostics.md. *)
AppExpr
    = AtomExpr AppSuffix* UsingClause?
    ;

UsingClause
    = "using" ( SatisfactionRef
              | "(" SatisfactionRef ( "," SatisfactionRef )* ","? ")" )
    ;

(* Block lambdas removed in v0.3. Trailing-block style replaced by
   single-expression arrow lambdas; for a multi-line body, write
   `(x) ->` followed by an indented block (v0.3.1). *)
AppSuffix
    = "(" ArgList? ")"
    | "." LOWER_IDENT ApplyTail?
    | "." UPPER_IDENT ApplyTail?
    ;

ApplyTail
    = "(" ArgList? ")"
    ;

ArgList
    = Expr ( "," Expr )* ","?
    ;

AtomExpr
    = LiteralExpr
    | UNIT_LITERAL
    | "Self"
    | "unreachable" ( "(" STRING_LITERAL ")" )?
    | "resume" "(" Expr? ")"
    | LOWER_IDENT
    | UPPER_IDENT
    | "(" Expr ")"
    | TupleExpr
    | ListLiteralExpr
    | LambdaExpr
    | IfExpr
    | MatchExpr
    | HandleExpr
    | DoExpr
    | StructLiteralExpr
    ;

LiteralExpr
    = INT_LITERAL | FLOAT_LITERAL | STRING_LITERAL | CHAR_LITERAL | BOOL_LITERAL
    ;

(* TupleExpr (v0.4): single production parallel to ListLiteralExpr.
   The mandatory first comma disambiguates `(x,)` (1-tuple) from `(x)`
   (parenthesised expression). *)
TupleExpr
    = "(" Expr "," ( Expr ( "," Expr )* ","? )? ")"
    ;

ListLiteralExpr
    = "[" ( Expr ( "," Expr )* ","? )? "]"
    ;

LambdaExpr
    = "(" ( LambdaParam ( "," LambdaParam )* ","? )? ")" "->" LambdaBody
    ;

(* LambdaParam (v0.4): same shape as FnParam — name plus optional type
   annotation and optional default value. Most callbacks omit both;
   types are useful for documentation / overload disambiguation. *)
LambdaParam
    = LOWER_IDENT ( ":" TypeExpr )? ( "=" Expr )?
    ;

(* LambdaBody mirrors BlockTail. After `->`, the parser checks whether
   the next physical line is indented more than the line containing the
   `->`: if yes, the body is the indented block; if no, it is a single
   expression continuation. See the bracket-suppression note in §1. *)
LambdaBody
    = Expr                                                         (* inline single-expr *)
    | NEWLINE INDENT BlockStmt ( NEWLINE BlockStmt )* NEWLINE? DEDENT   (* multi-line block *)
    ;

(* Block tail used after `=` (function and method bodies) and after `:`
   or `=>` (if/else branches, match case and handler clause bodies).
   May be inline (single Expr on the same line) or indented (NEWLINE
   then INDENT block). *)
BlockTail
    = Expr                                                         (* inline *)
    | NEWLINE INDENT BlockStmt ( NEWLINE BlockStmt )* NEWLINE? DEDENT   (* indented block *)
    ;

(* A statement of an indented block: an expression, or a local function
   declaration (v0.4.8, production added in v0.4.11) whose name is
   visible in the rest of the block. A local function takes no `pub`.
   The last statement of a block is its value and must be an Expr. *)
BlockStmt
    = FnDecl
    | Expr
    ;

(* IfExpr: `if cond: BODY ( else: BODY | else IfExpr )?`
   Inline form: `if cond: a else: b`
   Multi-line form: indented branches at the if/else's column. *)
IfExpr
    = "if" Expr ":" BlockTail ( "else" ( IfExpr | ":" BlockTail ) )?
    ;

(* MatchExpr: `match SCRUTINEE: cases`. Cases are newline-separated in
   the indented form; comma-separated cases are not accepted in v0.3.
   Inline single-case form is permitted but rarely useful. *)
MatchExpr
    = "match" Expr ":" MatchCaseList
    ;

MatchCaseList
    = MatchCase                                                     (* inline single case *)
    | NEWLINE INDENT MatchCase ( NEWLINE MatchCase )* NEWLINE? DEDENT
    ;

(* MatchCase (v0.4.3): supports or-patterns and guards. Or-pattern
   alternatives must bind identical names at identical types — checked
   in pattern_matching.md §5.5. The guard is a bool expression; if
   false, control falls through to the next case. *)
MatchCase
    = OrPattern MatchGuard? "=>" BlockTail
    ;

OrPattern
    = Pattern ( "|" Pattern )*
    ;

MatchGuard
    = "if" Expr
    ;

(* HandleExpr: `handle SUBJECT: clauses`. SUBJECT is a single Expr; for
   multi-line subjects, wrap a do-block: `handle (do: …): …`. *)
HandleExpr
    = "handle" Expr ":" HandlerClauseList
    ;

HandlerClauseList
    = HandlerClause
    | NEWLINE INDENT HandlerClause ( NEWLINE HandlerClause )* NEWLINE? DEDENT
    ;

HandlerClause
    = UPPER_IDENT "." LOWER_IDENT "(" PatternList? ")" "=>" BlockTail
    | "return" "(" Pattern ")" "=>" BlockTail
    ;

(* DoExpr: `do: BIND* FINAL`. Final form must be a non-bind expression. *)
DoExpr
    = "do" ":" DoBody
    ;

DoBody
    = DoFinalExpr                                                  (* inline single-expr *)
    | NEWLINE INDENT ( DoBindStmt NEWLINE )* DoFinalExpr NEWLINE? DEDENT
    ;

DoBindStmt
    = LOWER_IDENT "<-" Expr
    ;

DoFinalExpr
    = Expr
    ;

(* StructLiteralExpr keeps brace syntax — it is a record/struct
   literal, not a block. The braces here are the only remaining
   non-grouping braces in the language. *)
StructLiteralExpr
    = UPPER_IDENT "{" FieldInit ( "," FieldInit )* ","? "}"
    ;

FieldInit
    = LOWER_IDENT ( ":" Expr )?
    ;


(* ============================================================
   14. Comments and whitespace (lexer-level)
   ============================================================ *)

LineComment      = "//" (* up to end of line — discarded by lexer *) ;
Whitespace       = (* space; tab is a v0.3 error *) ;


(* ============================================================
   15. Open questions (carried forward)
   ============================================================

   Pre-resolved deferrals (lift into the indicated spec doc when drafted):

   Q8 — Type-variable introduction → spec/type_system.md
        See type_system.md §4.1 (Rule 4.1).

   Q10 — Numeric literal polymorphism → spec/type_system.md
        See type_system.md §5.7.

   Q11 — `mod`, `pub`, `in` keywords (closed)
        `mod` and `in` are reserved without productions. `pub` has a
        production since v0.4.11 (`Visibility`, §2).

   New in v0.4.11 (open, for the owner):

   N7 — Importing a function by name. Decided (v0.4.13): `UseSel`
        admits LOWER_IDENT, and the last LOWER_IDENT segment of a
        `UsePath` may name a function.

   N8 — Package scope for `using`. spec/type_system.md Rule 8.4 places
        package scope and the application root in a file named
        `package.fixed` (decided by the owner). There is no package
        declaration in the grammar.

   Q16 — `unreachable` → spec/type_system.md §5.6.

   N1(a) — Defaults at type-application sites → spec/type_system.md §7.3.
   N1(b) — QTT for `of` value-args → spec/quantities.md (TBD).

   Q6 — subsumed by N1; closed.

   New in v0.3 (status updated for v0.3.1):

   N4 — Multi-line lambda bodies (resolved in v0.3.1)
        Canonical form: `(x) ->` followed by an indented body. The
        off-side rule is re-enabled within the body even inside
        brackets.

   N5 — Multi-line handle subjects (accepted)
        The canonical form is `handle (do: …): …`. If the parens-
        around-do pattern proves verbose at scale, revisit with a two-
        block syntax such as `handle: SUBJECT  with: CLAUSES`.

   N6 — Tab handling (accepted)
        v0.3 errors on tab indentation. Revisit before bootstrap if
        community use shows this is too strict.

   ============================================================
   Appendix A — Implementation contract (normative)
   ============================================================

   The grammar above defines the *syntactic surface* of Fixed. This
   appendix defines two things every conformant Fixed parser must
   implement on top of that grammar:

     A.1  Error recovery — how a parser behaves on input that does
          not match the grammar.
     A.2  Trivia retention — what a parser must preserve about the
          space between tokens.

   Without these, two conformant parsers could produce materially
   different results for the same input — one halting on the first
   error, the other continuing — which would make tooling (LSP,
   formatters, doc generators, AI agents) non-portable.

   A.1 — Error recovery
   --------------------

   A conformant parser MUST NOT halt on the first syntactic error. It
   MUST produce a complete tree for the input it was given, with
   well-formed *gap nodes* (described below) standing in for productions
   it could not parse, and one diagnostic per failed production.

   A.1.1  Gap nodes

   Two AST node kinds carry recovery state:

     Error(recovered, span)
        A production that began but could not complete. `recovered`
        holds whatever subtrees were built before the parser bailed;
        often empty.

     Missing(expected, span)
        A required production that was absent entirely (e.g. `fn` with
        no name). `expected` is a grammar-level description of what was
        looked for. Span is zero-length, located where the missing
        token was expected.

   The exact node-class names are implementation choices; the contract
   is that the AST distinguishes "tried and failed" (Error) from "wasn't
   there at all" (Missing), and that downstream phases can recognise
   both.

   A.1.2  Synchronisation anchors

   When a production fails, the parser skips tokens until it reaches an
   *anchor* appropriate to its current parsing context, then resumes
   parsing the surrounding production. Anchors are reached but not
   consumed.

   The anchor sets, by context, are:

     Top-level item:
       `use`, `using`, `fn`, `fip`, `cap`, `data`, `effect`, `linear`,
       `type`, `mod`, `pub`, UPPER_IDENT (start of a satisfies
       declaration), EOF.

     Block body:
       NEWLINE at the block's indent, DEDENT, plus any top-level anchor.

     Argument list / tuple / list literal:
       `,`, the matching `)` / `]` / `}`.

     Type expression:
       `,`, `)`, NEWLINE, `=`, `->`, `with`.

     Pattern:
       `=>`, `,`, `)`.

     Match case or handler clause body:
       `=>` (recover the body), NEWLINE at the case's or clause's indent.

     `prop` / `forall` / `implies` body:
       NEWLINE at the body's indent, DEDENT.

   A.1.3  Anchor ranking

   Anchors are *ranked* by depth: the parser bails to the highest-ranked
   anchor whose context is currently active, never deeper. A missing `)`
   inside a function call does not eat past the enclosing `fn`'s
   NEWLINE. This keeps recovery local — at most one top-level item is
   damaged per syntactic error.

   A.1.4  Bounded skip

   Token-skip per recovery attempt MUST be bounded by the distance to
   the nearest in-context anchor. A conformant parser MUST NOT loop or
   restart from the beginning of input on error.

   A.1.5  Happy-path equivalence

   For input that matches the grammar, a conformant parser MUST produce
   no diagnostics and a tree containing no `Error` or `Missing` nodes.
   Recovery is observable only on ill-formed input.

   A.2 — Trivia retention
   ----------------------

   The grammar treats whitespace and `//` comments as insignificant to
   parsing. They are nonetheless significant to *tooling* (formatters,
   doc generators, LSP hover for adjacent comments). A conformant parser
   MUST preserve the following trivia in a side channel addressable by
   token offset:

     - LineComment(span, text)  — every `//`-comment, including its
       text content.
     - BlankLines(span, count)  — runs of two or more consecutive
       newlines (single newlines are reflected by NEWLINE / DEDENT
       tokens and do not need separate trivia events).

   The side channel is keyed on the start offset of the *next* real
   token; trivia is "leading" with respect to the token that follows
   it. There is no separate "trailing" slot in v0.4.7.

   Inline whitespace between tokens on the same line need NOT be
   preserved as trivia events; it is reconstructable from the source
   text given the token spans.

   `///` doc-comments are not yet classified separately from `//` line
   comments in v0.4.7; that is a future refinement.

   A.3 — Implicit DEDENT on outer-bracket waiter
   ---------------------------------------------

   When a re-engaged off-side body (lambda body, `match` block, `do`
   block, handler-clause block) inside a paren-suppressed context is
   followed on the same indent by a token the enclosing bracketed
   production is waiting for — `,` / `)` / `]` / `}` — the body
   terminates at that token. The DEDENT the scanner emits at the next
   physically-shallower line is absorbed by the enclosing production
   (typically the comma-separated list helper).

   This makes idiomatic code like

       sorted.get(mid).fold(
           (value) ->
               target.compare(value).fold(
                   () -> (lo, mid, hi),
                   () -> (lo, hi, mid),
               ),
           () -> acc,
       )

   parse as intended: the `(value) ->` body block ends at the `,`
   following the inner `.fold(...)`, not at a hypothetical DEDENT
   that the scanner emits later.

   Closely analogous to Python's behaviour inside parentheses.

   A.4 — Phase-by-phase tolerance
   ------------------------------

   The parser is the entry point for the contract, but conformance
   extends through later phases: every phase that consumes the AST MUST
   handle `Error` and `Missing` nodes (typically by emitting an
   "unresolved due to parse error" diagnostic and skipping the subtree)
   rather than crashing or silently producing wrong results. This is the
   compiler-wide form of the principle stated in
   `docs/plans/implementation-plan.md` §"Output design principles":
   *incremental and partial-result-tolerant by default*.

   ============================================================ *)
